---
import PageLayout from '@layouts/page.astro';
import Container from '@components/container.astro';
import Head from '@components/head.astro';
import Navbar from '@components/navbar.astro';
import Footer from '@components/footer.astro';
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import cover from '@assets/portfolio/office.png';

const study = {
  "title": "Platia Office Web",
  "period": "Fall 2025",
  "description": "Financial operations dashboard for managing users, wallet balances, deposits, withdrawals, transactions, audit logs, and internal admin workflows.",
  "coverAlt": "Platia Office Web sign-in screen",
  "skills": [
    "Frontend development",
    "Application modernization",
    "UI consolidation",
    "Container deployment"
  ],
  "tools": [
    "Angular",
    "TypeScript",
    "Tailwind CSS",
    "Angular Material",
    "Docker",
    "Dokploy"
  ],
  "slug": "office"
};
---

<PageLayout>
  <head><Head title={study.title + ' Case Study'} description={study.description} permalink={'/projects/' + study.slug} /></head>
  <body>
    <Navbar />
    <Container>
      <article class="project-detail">
        <header class="project-header">
          <h1>{study.title}</h1>
          <p class="project-date">{study.period}</p>
          <p class="tagline">{study.description}</p>

        </header>
        <figure class="project-cover">
          <Image src={cover} alt={study.coverAlt} widths={[480, 800, 1600]} sizes="(max-width: 864px) calc(100vw - 64px), 800px" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
        </figure>
        <section class="project-section at-a-glance" aria-label="Project details">
          <dl class="details">
            <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Software Engineer Intern</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Timeline</dt><dd>Sep 2025 - Mar 2026</dd></div>
            <div class="full-width"><dt>Skills</dt><dd>{study.skills.join(' / ')}</dd></div>
            <div class="full-width">
              <dt>Tech Stack</dt>
              <dd><ul class="tech-stack" aria-label="Technologies">
                {study.tools.map((tool, index) => <li class={'tech tech-' + (index % 4)}>{tool}</li>)}
              </ul></dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </section>
        <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="overview">
          <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
          <p>Office Web is Platia's financial operations dashboard for managing users, wallets, transactions, and other internal workflows. As a software engineer intern, I modernized its Angular frontend, integrated REST APIs, and helped deploy it across development and production environments.</p>
          <p>The application needed an updated structure and design, along with support for a newer version of its backend API. To make that happen, I learned how its Angular routes, components, and services fit together and mapped each API operation to the right user workflow.</p>
          <h3 id="key-features">Office Web Key Features</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Authentication and session handling</li>
            <li>User management and profile review</li>
            <li>Status update workflows</li>
            <li>Contact information updates</li>
            <li>Wallet balance and activity display</li>
            <li>Deposit and withdrawal operations</li>
            <li>Transaction review and approval</li>
            <li>Admin user management</li>
            <li>Role-based admin access</li>
            <li>Audit log tracking for admin actions</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="privacy"><Icon name="ion:lock-closed-outline" size={18} /><span>This case study shares a high-level account of my work.</span></p>
        </section>
        <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="takeaways">
          <h2 id="takeaways">Takeaways</h2>
          <div class="subsection">
            <h3 id="feature-analysis">Understanding the Workflows</h3>
            <p>Before changing the frontend, I needed to understand how administrators actually used it. Their roles and permissions shape what they can view and do, from reviewing an account to completing an action and checking its audit history. Seeing how those steps connect helped me build each screen as part of a larger workflow.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <h3 id="ui-consolidation">Simplifying the Frontend</h3>
            <p>The project used Tailwind CSS, Angular Material, Flowbite, and DaisyUI, which created overlapping styles and components. I consolidated the UI around Tailwind CSS and Angular Material, reducing runtime dependencies from 49 to 13 and components from 81 to 31. This taught me to question what the project really needed and reuse patterns where they made sense.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="subsection">
            <h3 id="financial-information">Making Financial Data Clear</h3>
            <p>Financial data has to make sense from the user's point of view. For example, an incoming transfer showed the recipient's number instead of the sender's name. Fixing it meant identifying the direction of the transaction and displaying the right person. It was a small change, but it made the activity much easier to understand.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <h3 id="deployment">Taking It Beyond Local</h3>
            <p>I also learned what happens after the code works on my computer. Using GitHub Actions, Docker, Dokploy, and Cloudflare Workers showed me how code moves through builds and into different environments. It also taught me to check API settings, deployment targets, and logs before assuming a problem came from the frontend.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <h3 id="remote-collaboration">Working Across Time Zones</h3>
            <p>On a remote team of three, I learned to investigate problems on my own, communicate when I needed context, and stay connected across time zones. Working with the product manager helped me prioritize what users needed, while backend discussions showed me how my frontend work fit into the larger system. One of my favorite parts was eventually meeting my teammates in person while visiting their city.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
        <nav class="back-button" aria-label="More projects">
          <a href="/portfolio"><Icon name="ion:arrow-back" size={18} /> Back to Portfolio</a>
        </nav>
      </article>
    </Container>
    <Footer />
  </body>
</PageLayout>

<style lang="scss">
  @use "../../styles/platia-project";
</style>
